/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1260:        %That is, the random embedding \(\psi\) is \((2C'/n)\)-vertex-spread.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1266:        %that is, the random embedding $\psi$ is $(2C'/n)$-vertex-spread.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1268:        %By Proposition \ref{prop:vertex_to_edge}, we conclude that there is a $(C''/n^{1/m_1(F)})$-spread distribution on subgraphs of $H$ which are isomorphic to $G$ (note that $m_1(F)=m_1(G)$). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1269:        %The \emph{moreover} part of the theorem follows from Theorem \ref{thm:FKNP}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1270:        %Moreover, if $p\ge K_{\ref{thm:FKNP}}C''\log n/n^{1/m_1(F)}$, then a.a.s.~$H_p$ contains an $F$-factor. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1272:    To complete the proof, by Proposition \ref{prop:vertex_to_edge}, it suffices to show that the random embedding $\psi$ is $(3C'/n)$-vertex-spread. Let $s\in [n]$. For every two sequences of distinct vertices $x_1,\dots,x_s\in [n]$ and $y_1,\dots,y_s\in V(H)$, we need to show that $\mathbb{P}[\psi(x_i)=y_i\text{ for }i\in[s]]\le {(3C'/n)}^s$.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1305:Thus the random embedding $\psi$ is $(3C'/n)$-vertex-spread. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1307:By Proposition \ref{prop:vertex_to_edge}, we conclude that there is a $(C''/n^{1/m_1(F)})$-spread distribution on subgraphs of $H$ which are isomorphic to $G$ (note that $m_1(F)=m_1(G)$).
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1308:The \emph{moreover} part of the theorem follows from Theorem \ref{thm:FKNP}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1309:\end{proof}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1314:\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_thm_factor}}\label{sec:robustness_F}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1318:In this subsection, we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:main_thm_factor} by applying Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1324:To verify the assumptions required in Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem}, we use a variant of Lemma~4.2 from \cite{lo2015f}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1344:    %With Theorem~\ref{main theorem} in hand, the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_thm_factor} follows a similar strategy to that of Theorem~\ref{main theorem-PM}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1346:    To have a more precise threshold for strictly 1-balanced $F$, we consider the spread properties of the \(F\)-graph of \(G\). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1348:    Note that \(G\) has an \(F\)-factor if and only if \(G_F\) contains a perfect matching. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1351:    %To prove Theorem \ref{thm:main_thm_factor}, we need the following result of Riordan~\cite[Theorem 18]{riordan2022random}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1353:    \begin{theorem}\cite[Theorem 18]{riordan2022random}\label{couple}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1361:Now we are ready to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:main_thm_factor}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1363:\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_thm_factor}]
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1370:It then follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:han_structural_theorem} that $(\mathcal{P},L^{\mu}_{\mathcal{P},F}(G))$ is $(2r-1)^r$-soluble, which implies the existence of a desired $F$-packing.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1375:We aim to apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem} to \(G'\) and \(\mathcal{P}'\) with \(\ell=1\) and \(D=5r^2\).
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1376:Recall that $\delta(F,1,5r^2)=1-1/{\chi}_{cr}(F)$ by Theorem \ref{thm:deltaFD}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1381:It remains to verify assumption (iii) of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1394:Therefore, assumption (iii) of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem} holds.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1395:We apply Theorem \ref{thm:main_technical_theorem} to $G'$ with $\gamma/2, \beta/2$ in place of $\gamma,\beta$, and obtain a $\left(C_1/n^{1/m_1(F)}\right)$-spread distribution on the set of $F$-factors in $G'$, which implies that a.a.s.~$G_p'$ contains an $F$-factor if \(p\ge Cn^{-1/m_1(F)}\log n\).
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1399:As there is an $\left(C_1/n^{1/m_1(F)}\right)$-spread distribution $\lambda$ on the family of $F$-factors in $G'$, it gives rise to a \(\left(C_1^{e_F}/n^{r-1}\right)\)-spread distribution $\lambda'$ on the perfect matchings of \(\mathcal{G}'\). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1405:Therefore, Theorem \ref{thm:FKNP} implies that a.a.s.~\(\mathcal{G}'_{\pi}\) contains a perfect matching if \(\pi\ge K_{\ref{thm:FKNP}}C_1^{e_F}\log n/ n^{(r-1)}=ap^{e_F}\). Thus, by Theorem \ref{couple}, a.a.s.~\(G_p'\) contains an \(F\)-factor.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1406:\end{proof}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1410:In this section we include a simple fact from the lattice theory which will be used to separate two families of robust index vectors later in the proof. 
