/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1443:\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM}}\label{sec:proof_main_result}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1445:In this section, we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1446:We first recall the structural framework developed by Gan and Han~\cite{gan2025keevash}, which reduces the existence of perfect matchings to a lattice-based solubility condition.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1447:After introducing the necessary structural tools, we present our algorithm and prove its correctness.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1496:Item~(2) shows that the exceptional set \(V_0\) has constant size, which allows the algorithm to search for a matching covering \(V_0\) by brute force. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1500:Moreover, the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:partition_l_degree} in~\cite{gan2025keevash} yields a useful converse to item~(4), which we record for later use: 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1506:in~\cite{gan2025keevash}, it is an immediate consequence of the way the sets \(V_1,\dots,V_s\) are constructed in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:partition_l_degree}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1514:%We begin by recalling the structural framework from~\cite{gan2025keevash}, which reduces the existence of perfect matchings to a solubility condition. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1516:Note that both the theorem below and Theorem \ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching} output a parameter $m$, which we use as an input to Lemma \ref{lem:partition_l_degree}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1518:\begin{theorem}\cite[Theorem 2.3]{gan2025keevash}\label{thm:gan_han_structural}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1528:Then $H$ contains a perfect matching if and only if $(\mathcal{P}, L_{\mathcal{P}}^{s,\mu}(H))$ is $(V_0, q)$-soluble. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1529:Furthermore, there is an algorithm with running time $O(n^{2^{k+2}}k^2)$ that finds a perfect matching in $H$ if $(\mathcal{P}, L_{\mathcal{P}}^{s,\mu}(H))$ is $(V_0, q)$-soluble.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1532:To apply Theorem~\ref{thm:gan_han_structural}, we also need a bound on the size of the relevant coset group. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1534:Although the original statement assumes all conditions of Lemma~\ref{lem:partition_l_degree}, its proof only uses (2) and (5), and moreover gives the additional bound $|L_{\max}^{d-s}/L_{\mathcal{P},1}^{s,\mu}(H)|\le (2k+1)^{d-s}$, which will be used later in the proof of the auxiliary theorem.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1540:The main new ingredient of the present paper is the following auxiliary theorem. It shows that once a suitable solution is found in the random subhypergraph, a.a.s. the remaining part of the hypergraph still contains a perfect matching. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1541:This theorem is the key tool that allows us to transfer the deterministic lattice-based framework of Gan and Han to the random sparsification setting. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1556:If $\vec{i}_{ \mathcal{P}}(V(H))\in L_{ \mathcal{P}}^{s,\mu}(H)$,  then there exists an $O(1/n^{k-1})$-spread distribution on the set of perfect matchings in $H$. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1557:In particular, if \(p\ge C\log n/n^{k-1}\), then a.a.s.\ \(H_p\) contains a perfect matching.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1561:%The algorithm for the decision problem of perfect matchings in $H$ (e.g. in \cite{gan2025keevash}) searches for all possible matchings $M$ of size at most $|V_0|+q$ such that \(M\) covers \(V_0\) and $\vec{i}_{\mathcal P}(V(H)\setminus V(M))\in L^{s,\mu}_{\mathcal{P}}(H)$ (and it is shown that this is sufficient). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1563:%If no such $M$ is found in $H_p$, then we can conclude that $H_p$ has no perfect matching.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1564:%However, if one such $M$ is found in $H_p$, we cannot argue as in the dense case by just showing $H-V(M)$ has a perfect matching. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1566:%That is to say, we need to work with the $p$-random sparsification of $H-V(M)$ with a small set of edges removed. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1571:%edges in \(E(\vec v)\) are then used to build an additional partial matching \(M_1\) in the algorithm. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1574:%Consequently, the exposure process only has a limited effect on the underlying structure of the hypergraph, so that the resulting hypergraph still satisfies the assumptions needed to apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1577:\subsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:algorithm_PM}}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1579:In this subsection, we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1580:First we present our algorithm for Theorem \ref{thm:algorithm_PM} (see Procedure \ref{alg:perfect_matching}). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1590:\Output{a.a.s.~$H_p$ has a perfect matching or none at all.}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1595:    Check whether $H_p$ has a perfect matching by brute force, return result and halt\;
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1612:            \Return{``a.a.s.~$H_p$ contains a perfect matching'' and halt}\;
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1619:\Return{``$H_p$ contains no perfect matching'' and halt}\;
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1622:%We are now ready to prove Theorem \ref{thm:algorithm_PM} assuming Theorem~\ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1624:\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM}]
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1625:We show correctness of Procedure \ref{alg:perfect_matching} and estimate its running time.  % Assume that \(p\ge K_{\ref{thm:FKNP}} C_{\ref{main theorem}}\log n / n^{k-1}\).
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1635:%We then check whether $(\mathcal{P},L^{\mu}_{\mathcal{P}}(H))$ is $\binom{2k-2}{k}$-soluble in $H$, which determines whether $H$ contains a perfect matching by Theorem \ref{thm:han_structural_theorem}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1640:By Theorem \ref{thm:gan_han_structural}, if $H_p$, as a subgraph of $H$, does not contain a matching $M$ covering $V_0$ which is a $(|V_0|+q)$-solution, then $H_p$ contains no perfect matching.  
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1641:The algorithm therefore first searches for such a solution. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1642:If no solution exists, the algorithm outputs correctly that no perfect matching exists. Otherwise, assume a $(|V_0|+q)$-solution is found. We show that a.a.s.\ it can be extended to a perfect matching using Theorem~\ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching}.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1658:By construction, for each \(\vec v\in I'\), we expose edges of type \(\vec v\) only at vertices outside \(V^+_{\vec v}\). Hence each vertex outside \(V_0\) is incident to at most \(\binom{d+k-1}{k}\eta n^{k-1}+|V_0|n^{k-2}\) revealed edges.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1695:\begin{proof}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1710:These yield \(h=(|V_0|+q)k\) edges in \(H_p\) containing \(v\), which are pairwise disjoint outside \(v\) by construction.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1713:\end{proof}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1724:Therefore, $H_p$ contains no perfect matching by Theorem~\ref{thm:gan_han_structural}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1727:Now suppose the algorithm finds a family of sets $(S_{\vec{v}})_{\vec{v} \in I'}$ satisfying~\eqref{eq:Sv}, and thus it halts and outputs that a.a.s.~$H_p$ contains a perfect matching.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1734:We construct a matching \(M_3\) greedily by assigning to each \(v \in \bigcup_{\vec{v}\in I'} S_{\vec{v}}\) one such edge, avoiding previously chosen edges and \(M_0 \cup M_1\). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1738:%\red{Therefore, it remains to show that a.a.s.~$H_p$ contains a perfect matching, which will finish the proof of the correctness of our algorithm.}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1743:\textbf{From a solution to a perfect matching.}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1744:% If no $(|V_0|+q)$-solution exists, then $H_p$ contains no perfect matching by Theorem~\ref{thm:gan_han_structural}. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1745:%Suppose $H_p$ contains a matching $M$ covering $V_0$ which is a $(|V_0|+q)$-solution and it remains to show that a.a.s.~the remaining $k$-graph has a perfect matching. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1746:Fix the matching \(M:=M_0\cup M_1\cup M_3\) constructed above, which is a \((|V_0|+q)\)-solution covering \(V_0\). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1747:It remains to show that a.a.s.~the remaining $k$-graph has a perfect matching. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1748:To this end, we verify that~\ref{item:(C*_degree)}--\ref{item:star} of Theorem~\ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching} hold for the remaining $k$-graph. 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1752:%either by \textcolor{blue}{the algorithm before it halts or by the auxiliary exposure used to construct \(M_3\).} 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1758:%Note that the algorithm never reveals any edge of \(H\) whose index vector lies in \(I_{\mathcal{P}}^{s,\mu}(H)\). 
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1774:%By construction, we have $|E(H)\setminus E(H')|\le O(n^{k-1})+\binom{d+k-1}{k}\cdot \eta n^{k}\le \mu n^k$,
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1793:Therefore, we can apply Theorem~\ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching} with parameters $\delta'/2$, $\mu/2$, $\sqrt{\eta}$, and $\beta/2$ in place of $\delta'$, $\mu$, $\eta$, and $\beta$, respectively, to $H'$.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1794:We then conclude that a.a.s.~$H_p'$ contains a perfect matching.
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1812:\end{proof}
/tmp/tmp.i55aPydxD9/main.tex:1816:\subsection{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:auxiliary_thm_perfect_matching}}
