553:%Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM} is obtained by combining our main technical Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem} with tools from~\cite{han2017decision,gan2025keevash}. 
555:A lattice-based framework was developed in~\cite{han2017decision, keevash2013polynomial} for deciding the existence of perfect matchings in hypergraphs under minimum codegree conditions. 
558:Later the framework was generalized to work under the minimum $\ell$-degree conditions \cite{han2020complexity, gan2025keevash}, and to deal with perfect \(F\)-packings in graphs~\cite{han2020complexity}. 
562:%For perfect matchings under general minimum \(\ell\)-degree conditions, however, the situation is more delicate.
564:%The work of \cite{gan2025keevash} managed to establish a  substantially more complicated partition lemma and used it to solve the problem under minimum $\ell$-degree.
566:%We defer these definitions and setup until Section~\ref{sec:proof_main_result}, where they are used in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm_PM}.
570:The key idea of the lattice-based absorption method is to reduce the search problem of perfect matchings to the search of \textit{constant-sized} matchings $M$ with certain divisibility constraint with respect to the partition $\mathcal P$ and the lattice $L$, whose existence can be determined in polynomial time.
571:%A common feature of lattice-based decision algorithms is that they search for a matching \(M\) of constant size such that 
572:%\(\vec{i}_{\mathcal P}(V(H)\setminus V(M))\in L,\) and the relevant structural theorems show that this condition is sufficient for deciding the existence of a perfect matching.
573:%Since \(M\) has constant size, such a search can be carried out in polynomial time. 
575:if no such matching is found in \(H_p\), then \(H_p\) contains no perfect matching.
576:However, if such a matching is found in \(H_p\), we cannot argue as in the dense case by just showing \(H-V(M)\) contains a perfect matching.  
578:Thus, instead of working with the \(p\)-random sparsification of \(H-V(M)\), we must work with the \(p\)-random sparsification of \(H-V(M)\) with a small set of edges removed.
586:We may therefore apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main_technical_theorem}, which yields a perfect matching in \(H_p\) a.a.s.
