+NOTES = r"""
+Paper grounding. Section 1.1 defines k-uniform hypergraphs, matchings, perfect
+matchings, minimum l-degree, and PM(k,l,delta). Theorem 1.2 says that above the
+perfect-fractional-matching threshold the dense decision problem is in P and
+even outputs a perfect matching or a negative certificate. Theorem 1.4 extends
+that tractable regime to random sparsifications. These positive results make a
+Track A claim in the paper's main dense regime untenable.
+
+Section 8 supplies the exact family used here. Immediately before Theorem 8.2,
+a q-solution is a matching of at most q edges whose deletion leaves the vertex
+index vector in a lattice. A (U,q)-solution additionally covers U and may use at
+most |U|+q edges. Theorem 8.2 makes (V_0,q)-solubility necessary and sufficient
+under its dense hypotheses. Procedure 1 then enumerates minimal matchings M_0
+covering V_0; lines 6--14 extend and test them. The running-time paragraph gives
+O(n^((k-1)|V_0|)) for this first enumeration and a larger polynomial bound for
+the complete search. This module asks for the same finite witness with U=V_0
+and q=0. It does not ask the checker to appeal to the asymptotic extension
+theorem.
+
+Construction. The instance has a special vertex s=V_0, g coordinate parts,
+good-tag and bad-tag parts, and a neutral padding part. For every projective
+start x there is one exceptional edge E(x): propagate x through g-1 displayed
+Mobius maps, then tag the edge G(x) exactly when the last map closes the cycle,
+and B(x) otherwise. Thus every coordinate label lies in exactly one exceptional
+edge, eliminating the planted-coordinate degree outlier. Core edges are all
+k-sets avoiding s whose good-tag count is even. Their index vectors generate
+L={z: sum(z)=0 mod k and z_G=0 mod 2}. The total number of vertices is a multiple
+of k and the good-tag part has odd size. Deleting E(x) therefore leaves an index
+vector in L exactly when E(x) carries a G tag.
+
+Inverse generation. Sample a projective start and the first g-1 invertible
+2-by-2 matrices. Compose them, choose a parabolic projective transformation
+whose unique fixed point is the sampled start, and solve for the last matrix so
+the cyclic product is that transformation. The planted edge is then the forward
+trajectory of the already sampled start. This is inverse generation, not a
+search over the finished instance.
+
+Step-0 cost comparison. A generic exact method composes the matrices and scans
+all p+1 projective starts, O(g+p); it succeeds and is reported separately as the
+Track B reference algorithm. At the shipping preset it performs 1,000,004
+fixed-point tests plus 96 elementary matrix operations. The compact route uses
+the same cyclic product but recognizes its parabolic conjugacy type and extracts
+the double fixed point directly, then propagates the answer, for at most 148
+field operations. The former is easy for a computer but not executable by hand;
+the latter is short if the invariant is noticed.
+
+Attacks. Per-coordinate exceptional incidence is exactly one, so the outlier
+probe has only a label tie and chooses zero. Zero-start propagation is wrong by
+construction. Uniform 256-restart sampling is tested against the exact bounded
+certificate language. The in-context affine ansatz replaces each projective map
+by the affine map agreeing at 0 and 1, solves that easier cycle, and then checks
+the resulting true trajectory; conditioned instances defeat it. The exact
+fixed-point scan succeeds as expected and is not placed among failing Track B
+attacks.
+
+Canonicalization. Independent projective relabellings of coordinate parts
+conjugate adjacent matrices; cyclic rotations and reversals reorder/invert the
+cycle. Coordinate-part padding sizes are genuine partition data but independent
+of the planted start. The key uses their sorted multiset together with the field
+and fixed part sizes, so it is invariant under these transformations and does
+not use the seed, answer, public coefficients, or rendered text. It is a strong
+cheap invariant for the succinct decorated hypergraph, not a complete
+canonization under every abstract hypergraph isomorphism.
+""".strip()
