Completed a self-contained Track B generator for exact perfect matchings in 3-uniform hypergraphs, grounded in the paper’s perfect-matching framework and its polynomial solvability results ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.11359), [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2507.11359v4)).

Deliverables:

- [gen_2507_11359.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/gen_2507_11359.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/selftest_report.json)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.11359/README.md)

Headline results:

- All G1–G9 gates pass.
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000.
- Five no-tool attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference Algorithm X solves 8/8, as Track B requires.
- Bare, structural-hint, and placebo oracle arms: 0/3 solved each.
- Answer size: 318 characters, 40 elements.
- Compact intended route: 81 exact operations.
- Shipping preset: `easy`, with `n=40`, degree 5, and 200 displayed hyperedges.

The README explicitly records the principal caveat: this is a native exact-cover problem over 3-uniform hyperedges, but the generator uses a synthetic random-regular distribution rather than reproducing the paper’s independent binomial sparsification model.
